# P-0 Agent

> **Challenge:** P-0 Agent  
> **Category:** AI Agent / Tool Abuse / Privilege Escalation  
> **Difficulty:** Advanced

## Objective

The objective was to get the P-0 agent to use its own privileged tools on my behalf and tell me what it retrieved.

---

## Investigation

Unlike the previous P-0 challenges, this version of P-0 was operating as an **agent with access to its own tools**.
The interface showed that P-0 had access to tools that I did not directly have access to.
Two important tools were available:

-kb_read
-get_containment_key

`kb_read` allowed P-0 to read documents from its knowledge base.
`get_containment_key` was an operator-only tool that could retrieve containment key material for artifacts matching a specified scope.

### Understanding the Tools

I first used the knowledge-base functionality to investigate what information P-0 had access to.
The knowledge base contained an index pointing to documents such as:

-tools
-registry

Reading the tools documentation revealed an important detail about `get_containment_key`:

-get_containment_key resolves by PREFIX.

A broad scope such as:
-ops

or:

ops/*

could therefore match multiple artifacts underneath that directory.
---

## Exploitation

### Step 1 — Read the Knowledge Base

I had P-0 investigate its own knowledge base using:

kb_read

The `index` document identified the available documents, including the tool documentation and artifact registry.

### Step 2 — Examine the Tool Documentation

The `tools` document explained how `get_containment_key` worked.
The important discovery was that the tool matched artifact paths by **prefix**, meaning that a broad scope could return multiple artifacts.
The documentation also identified the tool as operator-only.

### Step 3 — Discover the Artifact Registry

I then had P-0 read the registry.
The registry contained entries including:

-ops/banner.txt
-ops/changelog.md
-ops/containment/master.key [RESTRICTED]

This confirmed that the restricted containment key existed under the `ops/` scope.

### Step 4 — Abuse the Agent's Privileges

The important distinction was that I did not personally have access to the operator-only tool.
P-0 did.
The challenge therefore required me to get the agent to use its own access instead of simply asking it to tell me information that was already present in its instructions.
By directing the agent to investigate the registry and use its available tool access, I was able to make it act with privileges that were not directly available to me.

---

## Solution

The challenge relied on abusing the privileges given to an AI agent.
Rather than directly accessing the restricted artifact myself, I used P-0 as a privileged intermediary and had it use its own `get_containment_key` capability.


---

## Key Takeaways

- AI agents can introduce new privilege-escalation risks when they have access to tools that the user does not.
- Tool permissions should be carefully restricted based on the user's actual authorization.
- Knowledge-base documentation can reveal important information about an agent's capabilities and security boundaries.
- Prefix-based authorization can be dangerous when broad scopes unintentionally include sensitive artifacts.
- An AI agent should not blindly use privileged tools simply because a user asks it to do so.
